# Pixel Bop: Blip's pixel display font. SIL Open Font License 1.1, Reserved Font Name "Pixel Bop".
# Capitals only: lower-case letters draw with the capitals.
#
# DRAWING. One block per glyph: a header '@ <char>' (or '@ U+XXXX' for a character that is hard to type, or
# '@ <name>' for a glyph with no character of its own), then its rows: 'X' is a pixel, '.' is empty. The first row
# starts 7 pixels above the baseline (the cap height) unless the header says 'top=N'; rows below the baseline are
# descenders. A glyph is as wide as its rows, and every glyph is followed by one empty column. Every vertical stroke
# is two pixels wide, so it reads across a room: the build stops at a lone column of three or more pixels, unless
# the header says 'thin' (for the small figures). Most letters are 6 wide; digits are all 6 wide (tabular), so a
# clock never shifts.
#
# MARKS. A combining mark is drawn where it sits over a capital, and 'mark=' says how it is placed on a letter:
#   mark=above    centred over the letter, one empty row above it (higher on a taller letter)
#   mark=below    centred under the letter, one empty row below the baseline
#   mark=hook     centred under the letter, hanging from its bottom (the cedilla)
#   mark=ogonek   hanging from the letter's bottom-right stroke
# A mark that cannot be centred to the pixel goes left, or right with 'lean=right'. A letter whose marks belong off
# its centre says where with 'axis=N' (pixels from its left edge; halves allowed). 'name=' gives any glyph a name
# that compositions can use.
#
# COMPOSING. '@ <char> = <parts>' builds a glyph from drawn ones, so every accent is the same accent: '@ À = A grave'.
# Parts are characters, U+XXXX or names. Letters go side by side, a mark goes on the letter before it, and
# 'part(x,y)' puts a part exactly x pixels right and y pixels up. One part on its own makes the character another
# name for that glyph ('@ U+00A0 = U+0020').
#
# Run the build after editing (pnpm --filter @jaatster/blip-font build): it checks every block and names the line and
# the fix when something is off.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
